<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Pagus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kadifekale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Pagus, now Kadifekale, is the hill above the harbour of İzmir in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Around 300 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> refounded Smyrna on its slopes, and its summit carried the acropolis of the Hellenistic and Roman city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it held the upper citadel, which the Turks kept while the crusaders of 1344 held the harbour castle below.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-pagus.jpg" alt="Mount Pagus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr. E. Unlu · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kadifekale_IzmirTurkey_EUnluBlogspot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mount Pagus, Pagos in Greek, rises to 186 metres about 2 kilometres from the shore and commands a view over much of İzmir and the Gulf of İzmir. Its summit castle, Kadifekale, the velvet castle in Turkish, was first built in the 3rd century BCE. Strabo records that only a small part of ancient Smyrna stood on the hill, with most of the city around the harbour on the flat land below; the stadium and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> lay on the slopes just beneath the summit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded walls on the hill were the work of Lysimachos, a successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and later king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. They belonged to the refounding of Smyrna away from the old city on a small mound at the corner of the inner gulf, which could hold only a few thousand people. A legend told by Pausanias says that the goddesses called the two Nemeseis appeared to Alexander as he slept under a plane tree after hunting and told him to found a city there, and that the oracle at Klaros blessed those who would dwell on Pagus beyond the sacred Meles. The legend often appears on ancient coins.</p><p>The stadium west of the castle, scene of the martyrdom of St. Polycarp, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> east of the gates were rebuilt after an earthquake in 178; both are now built over, though traces of the theatre remain. In the 14th century the hill castle fell to the Aydinids while the port, with a castle of its own, was held by others until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a> took it in 1403. In the 19th century Kadifekale belonged to the Turkish core of İzmir on the slopes, while the city below was cosmopolitan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The present walls are medieval. Beside the castle lie Roman cisterns, renovated in Byzantine and Ottoman times, which formed the centre of Smyrna's water supply. The city began restoration work in 2007, and in 2020 the castle became a Tentative World Heritage Site as part of the Historical Port City of Izmir.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kadifekale” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In October 1344 its fleet captured the harbour castle of Smyrna, while the Turks kept the upper citadel on <b>Mount Pagus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/crusader-smyrna">Crusader Smyrna</a></li><li><blockquote>Antigonus and Lysimachus refounded Smyrna on the slopes of <b>Mount Pagus</b> c. 300 BCE, and it joined the Ionian League.</blockquote><a href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a></li></ul></section>
